1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:04,690
A lecturer points at the screen and says: look at this chart.

2
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:13,870
For most students, that sentence is enough. For a blind or low-vision student,

3
00:00:13,920 --> 00:00:18,610
it's a gap. The chart, its labels, its numbers: all of it is on screen,

4
00:00:18,660 --> 00:00:20,510
and none of it is spoken aloud.

5
00:00:24,400 --> 00:00:25,490
This is SeeHere.

6
00:00:28,300 --> 00:00:33,050
SeeHere takes a recorded lecture and adds short audio descriptions of what's on

7
00:00:33,100 --> 00:00:36,090
screen that the lecturer never reads aloud.

8
00:00:36,700 --> 00:00:41,050
Every description is linked to its evidence, placed in a natural pause,

9
00:00:41,320 --> 00:00:44,510
and reviewed by the instructor before anything is published.

10
00:00:46,199 --> 00:00:50,089
Here's how it works. The video is split into audio and frames.

11
00:00:50,899 --> 00:00:56,329
Speech is transcribed with word timings. Slide changes become keyframes.

12
00:00:56,379 --> 00:00:59,189
On-screen text is read with OCR,

13
00:00:59,239 --> 00:01:03,009
and a vision model on Vultr Serverless Inference finds the charts,

14
00:01:03,239 --> 00:01:04,569
tables and diagrams.

15
00:01:05,252 --> 00:01:07,562
SeeHere compares what's shown with what's said,

16
00:01:07,612 --> 00:01:12,682
and keeps the visuals that are never spoken. When the lecturer says this, or here,

17
00:01:12,772 --> 00:01:14,682
it works out which region they mean.

18
00:01:15,292 --> 00:01:19,522
Descriptions are written only from that evidence, every claim is checked,

19
00:01:19,632 --> 00:01:24,522
and a constraint solver places them in the pauses. Then the voice is generated,

20
00:01:24,692 --> 00:01:26,342
and the timing is checked again.

21
00:01:28,687 --> 00:01:34,377
Let's run it on the live site. I upload a 96-second lecture on photosynthesis,

22
00:01:34,787 --> 00:01:38,397
give it a title, and confirm I have permission to process it.

23
00:01:45,687 --> 00:01:50,557
Processing starts straight away. Each step is a real job on the server:

24
00:01:50,767 --> 00:01:55,097
preparing the media, transcribing speech, detecting slide changes,

25
00:01:55,307 --> 00:01:59,497
reading on-screen text, and asking the vision model what each slide shows.

26
00:02:00,387 --> 00:02:02,597
In under a minute, the lecture is ready.

27
00:02:19,287 --> 00:02:21,457
The player shows the original lecture,

28
00:02:21,607 --> 00:02:26,857
with each description voiced in a gap in the speech. Next to it is the transcript,

29
00:02:27,047 --> 00:02:29,417
with the descriptions placed where they'll be heard.

30
00:02:32,287 --> 00:02:36,617
Every description carries a verification state. Open its evidence,

31
00:02:36,687 --> 00:02:40,997
and you see where it came from: the keyframe, the region on screen,

32
00:02:41,127 --> 00:02:44,897
the text that was read, and what the lecturer was saying at that moment.

33
00:02:47,387 --> 00:02:51,737
One description is held back. The text reader got the equation wrong,

34
00:02:51,847 --> 00:02:55,177
so SeeHere won't voice it until the instructor checks it.

35
00:02:56,487 --> 00:03:00,577
The schedule shows where each description sits between stretches of speech.

36
00:03:01,067 --> 00:03:06,497
And everything exports: a WebVTT description track, a described video,

37
00:03:06,627 --> 00:03:11,457
the audio clips, an accessible transcript, and a coverage and verification report.

38
00:03:15,587 --> 00:03:19,277
What makes SeeHere different is that it doesn't trust itself blindly.

39
00:03:19,884 --> 00:03:23,094
Descriptions come only from evidence it can point to.

40
00:03:23,484 --> 00:03:26,954
Quoted text and numbers are checked against what was read on screen.

41
00:03:27,384 --> 00:03:30,394
A constraint solver, built on Google's OR-Tools,

42
00:03:30,444 --> 00:03:35,014
chooses which descriptions fit the pauses, so it never talks over the lecturer.

43
00:03:35,584 --> 00:03:37,274
After the voice is generated,

44
00:03:37,384 --> 00:03:40,674
the real clip lengths are measured and the plan is checked again.

45
00:03:40,984 --> 00:03:44,054
And when the evidence is uncertain, a person decides.

46
00:03:45,661 --> 00:03:49,311
All of this runs on Vultr. The app, the API,

47
00:03:49,741 --> 00:03:53,831
the database and the processing worker run on a Vultr Cloud Compute instance,

48
00:03:53,881 --> 00:03:55,791
served over HTTPS.

49
00:03:57,761 --> 00:04:00,911
The live status page shows the health of every part,

50
00:04:01,181 --> 00:04:05,971
the measured time of every stage, and every call to Vultr Serverless Inference,

51
00:04:06,021 --> 00:04:08,131
with its tokens and latency.

52
00:04:10,961 --> 00:04:13,561
SeeHere. Making every part of a lecture accessible.
